Thorough inspection of every component on the Office-to-Internet-Lab path
reveals no fault. All four Office PCs (enquiry 192.168.3.4, exam_cell
192.168.3.3, office_pc 192.168.3.2, tpo 192.168.3.5) successfully ping every
Internet Lab PC (pc2–pc5, 128.168.0.2–.5) with 0 % packet loss and TTL=62,
consistent with the expected two-hop path through router1 and router2. Routing
tables on all three routers are fully populated: router1 carries the RIP-learned
prefix 128.168.0.0/16 via 20.20.0.2 (eth3 toward router2), and router2 carries
192.168.3.0/24 via 20.20.0.1 (eth2 toward router1). RIP version 2 is correctly
configured and converged on router0, router1, and router2, with each router
advertising only its directly connected networks. No iptables rules exist in the
FORWARD chain on either transit router. Every end host has the correct IP
address, subnet mask, and default gateway. There is no injected fault, dropped
interface, misconfigured prefix, or access-control policy blocking the reported
flows. The helpdesk ticket describes a connectivity problem that does not exist
in the current network state; the Office department can reach the Internet Lab
computers without restriction. No corrective action is required on the network
side.
